SUBJECT: An old grand theatre building at night, seen from across an empty, rain-wet cobblestone street. The building is almost a silhouette against a deep blue twilight sky with a few faint stars and a thin crescent moon: a tall ornate facade with a curved pediment, columns and one arched entrance with two tall wooden doors, closed, with a thin line of warm amber light between and under them. Above the entrance hangs one large old sign: an empty dark board framed in iron, with dead neon tubes and no letters. One old street lamp on the left gives a weak, warm light. Puddles on the cobblestones hold faint reflections. Faded, peeling poster frames on the wall beside the doors, blank. Nobody is there.
COMPOSITION: The theatre stands in the middle of the picture, its sign in the upper middle. Plenty of dark empty sky above and empty wet street below. Calm, symmetrical, cinematic.
STYLE: A quiet painterly illustration like a hand-painted stage backdrop, soft brush texture, gentle film grain. Muted indigo, slate blue and one warm amber. The mood is solitude and tender nostalgia: a beloved old theatre waiting alone at dusk.
CONSTRAINTS: No text, no letters, no words on any sign or poster, no numbers, no logos, no signature, no watermark. No people. No 3D render look.
